 # Working notes: 72a6d8b343e4
 
-Record design decisions, hypotheses, review findings, and unresolved questions here.
-Imported associated notes retain original evidence/timestamps in `evidence/associated_notes.jsonl`.
+## Review scope and baseline
+
+No earlier return was used as a source lead; this review started from the packet's pinned baseline (`generator.py` SHA-256 `9d5b27c33d8162b1f6d23d89f5973ed8ce5031ad74cc827161bdc24e71ce5144`). The canonical Racket rule was treated as authority and was not edited.
+
+The baseline generated almost exclusively exactly-two, axially aligned colors. Those examples demonstrated line drawing but did not distinguish the intended rule from at least two simple rivals:
+
+1. connect aligned occurrences even when the color occurs three or more times;
+2. connect every exact pair, routing a diagonal pair with an L.
+
+It also omitted `RULE_HASH`, advertised controls that were not exact or observable, accepted unknown controls, missed all canonical profiles in the 60-draw profile check, and supplied no executable rival/witness contract.
+
+## Selected design
+
+Version `3.0.0` uses one object per color and constructs the grid from reserved footprints:
+
+- `horizontal` / `vertical`: exactly two separated, aligned markers; the full span is reserved;
+- `adjacent`: exactly two adjacent aligned markers; unchanged visually but still a lawful exact pair;
+- `diagonal`: exactly two markers sharing neither row nor column;
+- `triple`: one separated aligned pair plus a third marker aligned with neither endpoint;
+- `singleton`: one marker.
+
+Backtracking places all requested objects with disjoint reserved footprints. This avoids crossings, interior blockers, and order-dependent overwrites, while making every count recoverable from pixels alone.
+
+## Distribution choices
+
+- Grid dimensions are honestly bounded at `6..12`; every band accepts the same exact override domain.
+- Non-anchor defaults include one diagonal pair and one aligned triple. These are direct counterexamples to the two strongest canonical-fitting shortcuts.
+- At least one separated horizontal or vertical exact pair is generated by default, so routine outputs actually demonstrate the transformation rather than being all identity cases.
+- Exactly one slot in each consecutive block of three sample indices is a canonical-profile anchor. Anchors use an 8-row grid, width 9/10/11, exactly three foreground colors, and one of three simple layouts. This gives stable canonical support without allowing canonical-shape matching to dominate the distribution.
+- Difficulty changes default weights only. It never narrows the public control domain.
+
+Rejected alternatives:
+
+- **Only add rare negative samples.** Rejected because a lesson could still omit the rule-defining witness.
+- **Allow arbitrary overlapping objects and filter afterward.** Rejected because crossings can merge colors or obstruct a span, making controls non-observable and labels order-dependent.
+- **Make every sample canonical-shaped.** Rejected because it would trade one shortcut for another and reduce geometric diversity.
+- **Change the Racket rule.** Rejected: canonical outputs and rule-smoke parity already established that the authority implements the intended transformation.
+
+## Validation and executable semantics
+
+`observe_overrides()` independently reconstructs every control from pixels. `self_check()` validates shape, palette, multiplicity, geometry, span separation, and axis bounds. Unannotated grids with no separated exact aligned pair are rejected as non-demonstrative; an explicit override record permits intentionally requested no-op cases.
+
+The module declares two executable witnesses and rivals:
+
+- `exactly_two_only` versus `overconnect_multiples`;
+- `axis_alignment_only` versus `connect_diagonal_l`.
+
+`workspace/reference_probe.py` independently reconstructs positions, intended output, rivals, profiles, fingerprints, and distribution summaries without importing private generator helpers. It also checks the public canonical pairs through the portable reviewer helper.
+
+## Measured final state
+
+Final `generator.py` SHA-256: `ad4d602d1923dffec7a05ec0b436c5cd2c94ca7856062e4b5d914c3918fd5642`.
+
+- Full after battery: 12/12 green.
+- Task-local tests: 11/11 passed.
+- Rule smoke: 120/120 applied and fired, 0 identities, 0 crashes, 120/120 Python-mirror parity.
+- Canonical-profile floor: 22/60 exact hits; easy 6/20, medium 6/20, hard 10/20.
+- Semantic strict gate: 600/600 strict-valid.
+- Exact-control matrix: 1,071 cases passed across all bands.
+- Source-bound lesson census: 300/300 lessons materialized, 1,200/1,200 unique inputs, both declared rivals refuted in every lesson in every band.
+
+## Remaining limits
+
+The finite declared-rival checks do not prove universal induction, and the production lesson census does not rotate every pair into every query position. The task-local tests cover the two strongest simple rivals, exact controls, canonical support, and minimal impostors, but not every imaginable compound shortcut. The generator remains intentionally sparse and caps foreground object families at the advertised small counts.
+
+Imported associated notes, if any, remain in `evidence/associated_notes.jsonl`.
